From: Caboose <caboose@local>
Subject: [PATCH 92/93] Answer layout support, the instance version and
 swapchain requirements from the caches

A GL start through Zink asked the phone, in every process and for every layout it made,
whether a descriptor set layout was supported, and every process asked the executor's
instance version; each answer cost a round trip at the phone's latency. The virtual WSI
asked each swapchain image's memory requirements directly, past the requirements cache,
so every swapchain of every window cost one more round trip per image.

Descriptor set layout support is now answered from the executor session's capability
cache. Its answers depend on how the device was made, so they are kept in the physical
device's scope under the device's fingerprint: a digest of its creation (queues,
extensions in any order, features and each pNext structure's contents as the executor
receives them). A device made with a structure the encoder does not know, a device group
or a memory report callback has no fingerprint and always asks; the Vulkan loader's own
structures are not part of a creation. The question is the layout's flags, bindings,
binding flags and mutable descriptor types; immutable samplers, other structures and
output chains other than the variable descriptor count are always asked. An answer read
while the executor was lost is never kept, and a cached answer goes through the same
adjustment as an asked one.

The instance version has a scope of its own for the connection, so every process of the
session shares the answer one of them received; its log is named apart from the formats
logs a next session's warm-up reads. The virtual WSI's swapchain images take their memory
requirements through the requirements cache, as the executor answers them.

The requirements cache counts how each question was answered for the diagnostics line.
